<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catholicate of Abkhazia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Catholicate of Western Georgia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Catholicate of Abkhazia was the autocephalous church of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, headed by a catholicos who resided at Bichvinta (Pitsunda) and later at Gelati near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>. It took shape in the medieval kingdom of Abkhazia and continued under the kingdom of Imereti and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> principalities. The Russian authorities abolished it in 1814 and placed its dioceses under the Georgian exarchate of the Russian Church.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>When the Catholicate was established is uncertain. Some scholars place it in the 9th or 10th century, when the bishoprics of Abkhazia shifted their allegiance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a>. Titular catholicoi of Abkhazia first appear in sources of the 11th century, though they probably did not enjoy full independence. The first written account of the Catholicate dates from 1290, and some scholars place its foundation in the 13th century, when Mongol rule had divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and the western kings of Imereti were de facto independent of the Mongol Ilkhanid dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Full independence came in the 15th century, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>'s political fragmentation, since the kings of Imereti wanted a church of their own while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a> lay in their rivals' lands. The change is usually linked to the consecration of Archbishop Joachime of Tsaish and Bedia as catholicos by Michael IV, Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, during the reign of Bagrat VI (1463–1478); Michael's document, The Law of Faith, held that western and eastern Georgia had been converted separately. The catholicoi later took the title of patriarch, with dioceses such as Bichvinta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>, Gelati, Tsageri, Bedia and Mokvi.</p><p>In the late 16th century Catholicos Patriarch Eudemos I moved his residence from Bichvinta to Gelati, fleeing the Ottoman advance into Abkhazia; he carried out reforms and restored communion with the Patriarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> while keeping his independence. The catholicoi usually came from prominent noble families. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamization">Islamization</a> of Abkhazia, Adjara and Lower Guria under Ottoman rule dealt the church a severe blow, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> revived in Guria and parts of Abkhazia in the late 18th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> conquered Imereti in 1810.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The catholicoi regarded themselves as vicars of St. Andrew, who in medieval Georgian tradition had preached in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, the Colchis of classical writers, and their official style named Imereti, Odishi, Ponto-Abkhaz-Guria, Racha-Lechkhum-Svaneti, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ossetians">Ossetians</a> and the Dvals. After 1814 their dioceses remained in the Exarchate of Georgia until the unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a> regained its autocephaly in 1917. In 2009 an Abkhazian Orthodox Church was founded as a continuation of the Catholicate; it is not recognised as canonical within the Eastern Orthodox Church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Catholicate of Abkhazia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religious life centred on the Georgian Orthodox Church, whose western branch was headed by the <b>Catholicos of Abkhazia</b>, and Catholic missionaries also worked in the region in the seventeenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
